      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "The Constitution of India is the fundamental legal framework of the country.
    It establishes the structure of government, distributes powers among different
    institutions and levels of government, and sets out important rights, duties,
    and principles that guide the functioning of the nation. It also defines the
    relationship between citizens and the State and provides the constitutional basis
    for institutions such as Parliament, the executive, and the judiciary.\nAfter
    India became independent in 1947, the country needed a common constitutional
    framework for governing a large and diverse population. The Constitution was
    prepared through the Constituent Assembly, where its provisions were discussed
    and drafted before being finalized.\nThe Constitution was adopted by the Constituent
    Assembly on 26 November 1949. Its Preamble records that the people of India adopted,
    enacted, and gave themselves the Constitution on that date. Legislative.gov.in
    Most of its provisions came into force on 26 January 1950, a date the Constitution
    itself identifies as its commencement. Legislative.gov.in\nThe Constitution provides
    the framework within which laws are made and public institutions operate. It
    contains provisions concerning Fundamental Rights, government institutions, relations
    between the Union and the States, elections, constitutional authorities, and
    many other aspects of governance. Its Preamble expresses constitutional ideals
    including Justice, Liberty, Equality, and Fraternity. Legislative.gov.in\nThe
    Constitution therefore serves as the foundation of India\u2019s constitutional
    system, providing common rules and principles for governing the country while
    defining powers, responsibilities, and protections."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
